/* The engine's conceptual foundations, shared by its sidebar and topic index. */
(function(root){
 const foundations=[
  {id:'foundation:tgg',kind:'foundation',title:'Triple Graph Grammars',topic:'transforms',topics:['transforms'],copy:'Rules connect a source graph, a target graph and a correspondence graph that records how their elements relate.',body:'## How this supports the engine\nTriple Graph Grammars provide a foundation for specifying consistency between graph models. Correspondence links make the relationship between source and target elements explicit.\n\nThe transformation example shows source, correspondence and target graphs. TGG research also covers forward and backward transformations and incremental synchronization. The properties of a particular transformation depend on its rules.',references:['tgg-roadmap','tgg-correct']},
  {id:'foundation:lenses',kind:'foundation',title:'Graph Lenses',topic:'views',topics:['views','transforms'],copy:'A lens connects a graph with a view and defines how supported edits to that view are reflected back into the source.',body:'## How this supports the engine\nGraph lenses provide a foundation for keeping related graph views consistent. A forward operation derives a view. A backward operation uses the edited view and retained source information to update the source.\n\nRound-trip laws describe how these operations should agree. A view can omit information while a lens preserves the context needed to update the source. This explains bidirectional synchronization; it does not require every projection to be a one-to-one mapping.',references:['graph-lenses']},
  {id:'foundation:projections',kind:'foundation',title:'Graph Projections',topic:'views',topics:['views','transforms','ontology','stream'],copy:'A projection applies configured rules to inputs and produces its own graph and graph-change events.',body:'## How this supports the engine\nA projection is a configuration graph. It can select entities and relationships, transform attributes, combine inputs and propagate values.\n\nSeveral projections can use the same inputs under different ontologies. One projection can consume another projection’s graph or graph-change events, creating nested views. Inputs drive updates; an input that leaves a graph unchanged produces no graph diff.\n\nHealth modelling is one possible ontology. It does not define the engine’s scope.',references:['multi-view','stream-model']},
  {id:'foundation:bijective',kind:'foundation',title:'Bijective Graph Transformation',topic:'transforms',topics:['transforms'],copy:'A bijective transformation maps between defined sets of graphs one-to-one, so each result has exactly one inverse.',body:'## How this supports the engine\nBijective transformations provide a foundation for reversible graph mappings. Transforming a graph and applying the inverse recovers the original graph within the mapping’s defined domain.\n\nThis property must be established for the particular rules. A projection that combines several entities or discards information is not automatically bijective. Bidirectional synchronization is broader: it can use retained context to propagate updates without a unique inverse.',references:['bijective-lenses','tgg-correct']}
 ];
 root.atlasFoundations=foundations;if(typeof module!=='undefined')module.exports=foundations;
})(typeof window==='undefined'?globalThis:window);
